stale-input analysis (the real question):
- The shipped gate is LOUD: stale snapshot -> summary + CORPUS STALE + exit 1.
  Image mismatch is also loud (stderr + exit 2). Nothing silently green here.
- Only silent path is `--quiet --no-live-check` -> 0 bytes on stdout/stderr,
  exit 0 (verified on the current stale snapshot). But that requires explicitly
  disabling the live check; `--help` says "the snapshot may then be stale", and
  it is a deliberate offline opt-out, not a gate. Tests intentionally cover
  live_check=False as a by-design path.
- `--quiet` alone does NOT suppress staleness: stale sets `stale`, so the
  warning prints and exit is 1. So no default/likely gate invocation is silent.
- progress_report does not read the fn-audit snapshot at all (grep = 0); l3check
  does not either; test_plan_check.py does not exist yet (H59). They neither
  notice staleness nor pass on it — they are simply not gates over it.
- Only committed data artifacts that feed gates: fn-audit snapshot (H69 owner),
  tools/golden/eu2011.json (l2check fails LOUDLY on decoder drift; --update-golden
  is the only refresh, AGENTS forbids using it to make a failure pass),
  tools/fixtures/fn_match_eu_us_2011.json (M1.28 fails loudly on US mismatch).
- README's cited snapshot SHA (4606aa2a…78fe38) matches the file on disk
  (sha256sum 4606aa2a…78fe38): the committed artifact is internally consistent.

verdict:
- No gate silently passes on stale input. Freshness is H69's scope (refresh +
  moved-warn/new-gone-fail policy + a fresh-snapshot byte-identical check in its
  Done-when). H59/H60 trace plan->board and ID resolution, not artifact
  freshness, so they do not need to know about it. Per step 4, this is covered
